to-naval-ravikant-source = lines-of (cons "# Naval Ravikant"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Avik Jain is not writing to you as an investor. He wrote that he seeks you “not as investor (for being financially free to do as I please) but as peer who inspired the path”. This page sets out why, and it credits you in full for every idea of yours the work builds on."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What the startup world took from you, and what it left behind"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Most people read *The Almanack* as advice: get leverage, build specific knowledge, play long games. What they skip is that its two halves, wealth and happiness, rest on one theory of knowledge. You took that theory from David Deutsch and held to it: knowledge grows by conjecture and error correction; a good explanation is hard to vary; problems are soluble. On your podcast with Brett Hall you went through *The Beginning of Infinity* chapter by chapter, and you followed Deutsch's claim that one person can understand the foundational theories: epistemology, evolution, quantum theory and the theory of computation."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Your economics follows from that epistemology. Specific knowledge is knowledge that cannot be trained, because it is created. Leverage is what lets one person's created knowledge act at scale, and the strongest leverage you named is products with no marginal cost of replication, code and media; you called code probably the most powerful form of permissionless leverage. Stated plainly, your thesis is that wealth is knowledge made into a form that copies for free and acts without its author present. The startup world uses the advice and ignores the thesis. Hyperactivity starts from the thesis."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity is, in your terms"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Hyperactivity is in Avik's words a language and “a computational model of reality i.e. wolfram's ruliad executing on silicon”, with cubical type theory at its core"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ""
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Code and media become one leverage.** In its content-addressed store a definition is identified by its content, and a name is only a view of it"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p2 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". The store holds not only programs but their proofs, semantics and equivalences"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p6 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ", and the paper itself is held there: the paragraph each link on this page opens is a definition of that store. A peer can send a definition with its dependency closure and its proofs, and the recipient checks and uses them in its own context"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p7 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". Writing and code copy for free, and they compose with their evidence attached."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**A good explanation gets a formal statement.** In the paper a reading determines the family of histories compatible with it"
  (cons (cite s1-p8 "(1.1)")
  (cons ", and what it warrants is a construction over every one of them"
  (cons (cite s1-p12 "(1.3)")
  (cons "; a conclusion is supported exactly when that construction exists, and refinement never takes it away"
  (cons (cite s1-p13 "(§1)")
  (cons ". Read as Deutsch's criterion: an explanation that holds over the whole family cannot be varied by choosing a different compatible history."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Error correction becomes structural.** An edit's effect on everything that depends on it is computed by the dependency operations the store already keeps"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p6 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ", and a proof whose statement no longer applies does not silently survive the edit: its type changes and must be inhabited again"
  (cons (cite s10-11-p6 "(§10.11)")
  (cons ". Correcting one thing cannot quietly leave another resting on the old version."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**The foundations are one structure.** The theory of computation is the language. Epistemology is its account of observation and warrant"
  (cons (cite s1-p12 "(§1)")
  (cons ". Communication is a relation between participants' states and histories"
  (cons (cite s1-p20 "(Receive, 1.4)")
  (cons ". And identity by content rests on a stated assumption, the collision resistance of the digest, kept explicit rather than hidden"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p3 "(§10.8)")
  (cons "; Avik adds that the interaction system “can model crypto primitives & hardness assumptions as well”."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Specific knowledge gets a home.** Your advice was to find the knowledge only you have and give it leverage. Avik did that for ten years outside academic publishing; he turned down a PhD, a $1.2M offer from Jane Street and an offer from OpenAI to build one product whose interest is total. In the store every reuse refers to the exact definition it uses, so a reuse never loses what it rests on"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p2 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ""
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Wealth and happiness, one structure"
  (cons ""
  (cons "You put wealth and happiness in one book because you hold that the same clear seeing produces both. Avik holds the same. He studied with Dacher Keltner, the science of happiness and prosociality, and holds that hyperactivity is a technological work determined by the science of human happiness."
  (cons ""
  (cons "His political path runs alongside yours. In his words: “I was an anarchocapitalist when I started this work and im an anarchocommunist now, so all is well regardless”. A store of free, checked, composable knowledge serves both readings: the individual gets total leverage, and the commons gets everything, owned by no one, credited to everyone."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Why you, and why now"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Avik describes successful venture capitalists as “a class of humans oriented the opposite way without any deep expertise but deep receptivity to expertise”. His account of how understanding grows, “depth & intensity with openness & detachment from perspectives contradicted by reality”, is your account of reading and Deutsch's account of criticism. Your work with Deutsch shows that receptivity: you took a physicist's epistemology seriously enough to rebuild your thinking on it. Hyperactivity asks for the same reading, from someone who will look for the best in it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Everything here that grows from your thinking, leverage as the zero-marginal-cost replication of created knowledge, specific knowledge, the unity of wealth and happiness, and your carrying of Deutsch's epistemology into the world of builders, is yours, credited to you in full."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Naval Ravikant, Wikipedia, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naval_Ravikant"
  (cons "- Find a position of leverage, The Almanack of Naval Ravikant, https://navalmanack.com/almanack-of-naval-ravikant/find-a-position-of-leverage"
  (cons "- We're at the beginning of an infinity of knowledge, nav.al, https://nav.al/knowledge"
  (cons "- A single human can understand everything important, nav.al, https://nav.al/theory"
  (cons "- David Deutsch and Naval Ravikant, The Tim Ferriss Show, https://tim.blog/2023/03/24/david-deutsch-naval-ravikant/"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Part I, the blocks linked above"
